RAID-4 involves having N data disks (N >=3D 2) and 1 parity disk.  The pa=
rity=20
disk contains the XOR of the blocks on the N data disks.  If one of the N=
=20
disks dies then it's contents can easily be regenerated by the XOR of the=
=20
surviving N-1 disks and the parity disk.
RAID-5 is the same but has the parity data spread across all the disks fo=
r=20
best performance.  Thus RAID-5 has 3 or more disks.

In RAID-4 or RAID-5 if you lose two disks at the same time then the XOR w=
on't=20
get your data back and you are comprehensively stuffed.

If you create a RAID-5 with two partitions on the same disk then you may =
as=20
well use a bulk eraser.

The scheme I mentioned in my previous message is the simplest way of doin=
g=20
this with such disks.  But really if your time is worth more than about $=
10=20
per hour you should just buy some more 80G disks.

